# What must NOT reach the build context.
#
# Two separate concerns are in this file and they are not equally serious.
# Most lines are size: the Dockerfile does one `COPY . /app`, so anything
# left here is uploaded to the daemon and then baked into a layer. A few
# lines are DISCLOSURE, and those are marked -- a build context is not a
# private thing, it lands in an image that gets copied to hosts.

# --- Disclosure ------------------------------------------------------------

# `.env.local` holds this machine's NOCTORNAL_TOTP_KEK, and losing or
# leaking that key is the whole ballgame: it seals every TOTP secret at
# rest. Baking it into a production image would ship a development key to
# a server AND put the dev database DSN one `scripts/_env.py` call away --
# and `load_env_local()` only declines to override variables that are
# already set, so a variable compose forgot would silently come from here.
.env
.env.*
.env.local

# At any depth, and with an editor's copy of one (2026-10-03, g48
# verification). The three lines above are root-anchored, as every line in
# this file is, while .gitignore's `.env` and `.env.*` match at any depth, so
# apps/api/.env.local and infra/production/.env.production were gitignored
# yet reached every image; and `secrets.env.bak`, `secrets.env~` and
# `secrets-prod.env.save`, which an editor or an operator leaves beside the
# real file, were in neither list. `*.env`, `*.env.*` and `*.env~` take all
# of them; the tracked `<name>.env.example` templates are put back (the `?*`
# keeps a bare `.env.example` out, as `.env.*` always did). .gitignore
# carries the same set, and apps/api/tests/test_g48_image_context.py holds
# the two lists to each other in both directions.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
**/*.env.*
**/*.env~
!**/?*.env.example

# Renders of real panes against real case data. A screenshot of an
# AMBER_STRICT case carries that case's classification and does not look
# like it does (the same reasoning as .gitignore).
screenshots/
shots/

# --- Secrets that live under infra/production ------------------------------

# The operator's real secrets file sits beside the compose file. It is
# gitignored, which means nothing stops it being COPYed into an image.
infra/production/secrets.env

# S2 (2026-09-24) moved the egress keys and the worker and egress database
# passwords out of secrets.env into three files beside it, and this list
# never learned them: every image carried the BYPASSRLS role's password and
# the egress proxy's private seal key (infra-1, 2026-10-03). docs/17 F52
# (2026-10-02) found the same of the schema owner's password
# (postgres-init.env) and DSN (migrate.env): each was baked into the image
# every runtime container runs, so a process meant to hold the request role
# could read the owner's DSN off its own disk. The pattern below takes every
# `*.env` in the directory, so the next secret file added there is excluded
# without anyone remembering to list it; the `.example` templates end in
# `.example` and are not matched (the `**/*.env.*` rule above would take
# them, and the `!` line there puts them back on purpose: they hold
# placeholders only, and a test holds them in the context). The names are
# listed as well because a pattern is easier to get wrong than a name, and
# apps/api/tests/test_g48_image_context.py holds every gitignored secret
# path under infra/production to this file.
infra/production/*.env
infra/production/egress-proxy.env
infra/production/egress-client.env
infra/production/postgres-init.env
infra/production/migrate.env
infra/production/caddy.env

# The backups scripts/production_secrets.py takes of every secrets file
# before it changes one (`NAME.backup-UTCSTAMP`, each holding what the file
# held) and its temporary file (`.NAME.tmp-PID`, a whole new secrets file for
# the instant before it is renamed over the old one, left behind only by a
# kill or a power cut). `**/*.env.*` above takes both by their shape already;
# they are listed here by the shape .gitignore lists them in, so the two
# files stay each other's mirror.
infra/production/*.env.backup-*
infra/production/.*.tmp-*

# Private keys and certificates, wherever they are in the tree. tls/ (below)
# is the one the deployment uses; these catch a copy somebody made.
**/*.key
**/*.pem

# An editor's or an operator's copy of any file (`~`, `.swp`, `.bak`, `.orig`,
# `.old`, `.save`): a copy of a file protected above keeps its contents under
# a name no rule knows (2026-10-03, g48 verification). No tracked file ends in
# one of these, and .gitignore carries the same list.
**/*~
**/*.bak
**/*.orig
**/*.old
**/*.save
**/*.swp

# Database dumps and backups (infra-2, 2026-10-03). The documented backup
# once wrote ./noctornal-<timestamp>.sql into the repository root, which is
# the build context, and the next `up -d --build` copied the whole case
# database in plaintext into every container. Root-level patterns on
# purpose: a `*.sql` that matched at every depth would drop db/init and
# db/schema.sql from the image. The documented backup goes OUTSIDE the
# checkout (infra/production/README.md); this is the net under it.
*.sql
*.sql.*
*.dump
*.dump.*
noctornal-*
backup/
backups/

# MinIO's TLS keypair, including `private.key`. The compose file BIND-mounts
# this directory into the containers that need it, so nothing is lost by
# excluding it -- and without this line the private key of the production
# object store would be baked into every layer of the image and travel with
# it to wherever the image goes.
infra/production/tls/

# --- Size and staleness ----------------------------------------------------

# A host virtualenv is the wrong platform's binaries (this repo is
# developed on Windows) and would shadow nothing useful -- the image
# installs its own.
.venv/
apps/api/.venv/

.git/
.github/

# Build artefacts of a DIFFERENT distribution channel. `release/` is the
# hand-installer path (install.sh / INSTALL.md), which a container
# deployment does not use, and the packaged zip is a copy of the whole
# repository -- committing it into an image doubles the layer for nothing.
release/
dist/
*.zip

node_modules/
.next/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# YARA rule sources are cloned into these two directories by
# scripts/yara_db.py. `yara/sources.json` and `yara/README.md` are the
# tracked configuration and DO belong in the image; the clones do not.
yara/vendor/
yara/dist/

# Editor and agent noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
